@@ -74,6 +74,26 @@ km
 ...
 ```
 
+## Logarithmic units
+
+Some units carry values on a logarithmic scale instead of a linear one. Prepend the
+`@logarithmic` decorator to a derived unit definition to declare one. Its first argument is
+the scale, the optional second one is the base of the logarithm (ten by default). A value
+`x` in such a unit denotes `reference · base^(x / scale)`, where the reference is the
+defining expression:
+``` numbat
+@logarithmic(10)
+@aliases(dBm: short)
+unit decibel_milliwatt: Power = 1 milliwatt
+
+1 W -> dBm  # 30 dBm
+```
+Metric prefixes divide the scale, which is why a decibel is ten times finer than a bel.
+Such a value is called a *level*. Multiplying or dividing a level by a plain number, and
+negating it, scale the level itself; everywhere else a level stands for the linear quantity
+it denotes. A logarithmic unit can not appear inside a compound unit, and no unit can be
+defined from a level.
+
 ## Ad-hoc units
 
 It is often useful to introduce 'fictional' physical units (and dimensions).
